\f0\b\fs20 \cf0 Some Thoughts about My Father, Professor R. Narasimhan
\f1\b0 \
\
My father, Rangaswamy Narasimhan, grew up as the third of four siblings in a close-knit family in Madras (Chennai), and throughout his life family\'97nieces, nephews, aunts, uncles, cousins\'97remained very important to him. If he found himself nearby while traveling, he would make a point of paying his respects, and he was generous of his advice and even financial support to the younger generation as they made their way through the world. This was equally true of the families of his close friends. Indeed, my father was notable for his rapport with all children of all ages, never, ever talking down to them. He was always happy to join in, or even initiate, a board game or a simple game with pencil and paper. It was unfortunate for all of us that he had limited time with his grandson, Adam, but they enjoyed each other\'92s company as opportunity allowed. My father took a very young Adam from one end of the zoo to the other, as he had done with me. Some years later he was proud to share Adam\'92s early forays into coding, in particular, a multi-speed game in Visual Basic that challenged the player to navigate mazes with a constantly moving square, and predictably exploded the head of many an adult. \
\
Of his siblings, my father was particularly close to his younger brother, Rangaswamy Srinivasan, with whom he shared the pull of the sciences. Srinivasan went on to make his career in photochemistry, inventing the technique of ablative photodecomposition that underlies LASIK eye surgery, for which he was awarded the National Medal of Technology and Innovation by President Obama. My father encouraged his brother to follow him to the United States for graduate studies, but was clear-eyed about the personal challenges that this would entail. In a letter home, he sternly cautioned his parents to hold back on criticism of his brother\'92s grades or lifestyle as he was settling into new routines: \'93When to strange faces are added the inevitability of strange food, strange social mores, and strange language (it is really surprising how disconcerting talking in English can at first be when one is forced to realise that one can\'92t fall back on \ul any other\ulnone  language!) the first reaction would seem to be one of extreme irritability. Even minor discomforts and accidents tend to get magnified into major persecutions.\'94 My father estimated that this \'93transitional stage\'94 could continue for up to a year, by which time \'93the new set up should have become a sufficiently strong habit to permit launching out on new endeavours.\'94 But Srinivasan, for his part, has noted that my father\'92s personality was deeply and permanently affected by the six long years (1948-54) he spent thousands of miles from home, when letters took weeks to travel in either direction and hearing a familiar voice meant waiting all day by the telephone for the call to be put through.\
\
My paternal grandmother passed away before I was born, but by all accounts she was a strong and charismatic figure. She herself played the piano, and she encouraged my father to learn the violin. He took naturally to Carnatic music, but when his teacher recommended a more intensive schedule of lessons, the family decided his school studies should come first. That didn\'92t stop my father\'92s pursuit of music, of course. My uncle describes an occasion during World War II, when they were both enlisted as reservists. A European orchestra was performing Beethoven for the British troops at Fort St. George, and my father told his brother they had to go. \'93How will we get in?,\'94 his brother objected. \'93If we\'92re wearing our uniforms no one will stop us,\'94 he replied. And sure enough, they went boldly forth and enjoyed the concert! \
\
My father had hoped that the United States would afford him the opportunity to go back to the violin, but it was not to be. Many years later, when I was learning the violin as a child, he confided his continued regret that he did not do so. In the meantime, though, his artistic propensities found other outlets. He excelled in watercolour classes at school, and his pencil sketches throughout his life convey a sense of movement, force, and dimensionality. He was also a voracious reader and a prolific writer. Surviving correspondence from his years at CalTech and Bloomington shines a light on earnest discussions carried on over many months and many thousands of miles with his friends from the Engineering College at Guindy. The young men wrestled with questions of reality vs. appearance; the hierarchical relationship between aesthetics, art, and beauty; the artistic vs. communicative functions of language; the camera as an agent of art vs. artifice; the implication of the transcendental ultimate; and of course, the nature of Truth. They discussed a new film of 
\f2\i Hamlet
\f1\i0 , Walt Disney\'92s 
\f2\i Cinderella
\f1\i0 , the pessimism of Norbert Weiner, Freud, Aquinas, the Upanishads, Gertrude Stein, and always James Joyce. Clearly, my father worked out his thoughts on paper. Pen and paper also allowed his imagination to flow. While at Bloomington, he wrote for a student publication, and he left us a sheaf of short stories, Kafka-esque fables, poems and limericks, as well as a \'93Peripatetic Guide\'94 to America for the newly arrived Indian.\
\
Bookshops and record shops were his natural habitat, at home and on his travels; the Melodia record shop was a regular haunt of ours in Bombay. My father collected records from an early age\'97his letters to his brother provide detailed recommendations on which recordings to buy\'97and he became an early adopter of the Walkman cassette player, delighted that the portability of the new medium allowed him to stock up on music when he traveled abroad. My lasting memory of his 9th-floor flat in the TIFR Housing Colony is watching the sun set over the waves to the accompaniment of his record-player: Bach, Schubert lieder, Mahler symphonies, jazz, Tom Lehrer. I have no doubt that my decision to take up the cello in later life was influenced by hearing Pablo Casals playing Bach\'92s solo suites by the Arabian Sea. \
\
Looking back, it was this basic instinct for the arts, musical and visual, and interest in language in all its forms, for work and for play, that drew my parents together and characterises my memories of our family. My mother, Sita (n\'e9e Thiruvenkata Chari), was a university lecturer in English literature and in Comparative Religion. Early in their courtship my father took her to see 
\f2\i Bridge over the River Kwai;
\f1\i0  \'93You have to see this film!,\'94 he declared. Ever after, the cinema was a standard family outing. My father\'92s tastes ranged wide. He admired Walt Disney and the Pink Panther as much as Ingmar Bergmann and Stanley Kubrick, and he introduced me to films as varied as 
\f2\i Born Free, The Godfather,
\f1\i0  and 
\f2\i A Chorus Line.
\f1\i0 \
\
I remember lingering at the dining table over late family breakfasts, the conversation ranging over everything and nothing with plenty of word play and silly humour. And I remember family holidays associated with my father\'92s professional trips to Europe, especially Italy. Near Gubbio, we rode up a mountainside in what was basically an open metal basket suspended from a cable, my father safeguarding the family with his hand firmly clutching the gate shut. In Pisa, my parents took turns taking me up the Leaning Tower every day (for ten days). A summer in Rome included an open air performance of Verdi\'92s 
\f2\i Aida
\f1\i0  at the Baths of Caracalla. On another visit, staying in the British Academy flat near the Vatican through my mother\'92s professional connections, she taught us Double Bezique, and each evening after dinner we sat down to play cards. We all endured my father\'92s long-term quest to see every painting by Titian. And then there were the innumerable expeditions to catch views of or from mountain tops that were invariably shrouded in clouds.\
\
My father knew he belonged in India, his U.S. sojourn had convinced him of that, but he never passed up an opportunity to travel to new places, to see the sights, meet the people, taste their cuisines, visit their places of culture and worship. His was a boundless and open curiosity informed by a scientist\'92s mind. In 1981, while spending some months in India, I was offered the opportunity to visit China. I flew out from Bombay, and my father sent me off with a project: \'93See how their One Child policy is working.\'94 \'93How would I know?,\'94 I asked. \'93When you go somewhere, count how many children there are running around,\'94 he replied matter-of-factly. And I thought, \'93Of course.\'94\
\

\f2\i Tanjam Jacobson
